EVERY PC-RELATIVE DATA-WORD LOAD HALFWORD.

Experimental English Major C ARMv7-A T32 architecture specification.
Exactly and only the finite 16-bit halfword region 0x4800–0x4FFF.
A closed native encoding classification, not a complete ARM ISA
and not a claim that these instructions ran on a physical device.

NORMATIVE ARCHITECTURAL REFERENCE.

Arm Limited, ARM Architecture Reference Manual, ARMv7-A and ARMv7-R,
ARM DDI 0406C.d (ID040418), A6 16-bit Thumb instruction decoding
and A8 LDR (literal), T1 encoding.
The full definition also depends on A3 memory types,
permissions, alignment, exceptions and access ordering.

Official ARM reference identification:
https://developer.arm.com/documentation/ddi0406/cd

The complete source-data enumeration is in the repository at:
_/fixtures/armv7-a-t32-pc-relative-word-load-halfwords.tsv

The currently named source operation is number 41:
"Load a thirty-two-bit word from an architecture-defined
literal address based upon the current instruction's
PC-relative value."
The sibling closed operation grant supplies its exact
permitted ARM T1 encoding restriction and must be read
with the machine and memory-contract documents.

PART ONE. THE NATIVE TWO-BYTE INSTRUCTION AND ITS FIELDS.

The highest five bits of every halfword in this region
are binary 01001.

The next three bits choose an integer register number
from zero through seven, indicating the destination
register that will receive the loaded word.

The eight lowest bits are an unsigned integer, imm8,
from zero through 255 inclusive. The encoded BYTE
offset is precisely FOUR TIMES imm8, giving a number
between zero and 1020 inclusive in multiples of four.

The fixed prefix plus the 3-bit destination field
and 8-bit immediate account for:
    eight destination choices times 256 immediate choices
    = 2048 different 16-bit halfwords.

Every one of the 2048 halfwords 4800–4FFF
is assigned to the T1 LDR (literal) encoding.
All are TWO bytes long and do not consume
a second halfword as part of the instruction.

The instruction has no encoded ordinary
data-source register other than the architectural
PC-relative address mechanism.
It has no encoded branch target register and
no flag-setting selection bit.

PART TWO. HOW THE MEMORY ADDRESS IS CALCULATED.

Let the current instruction begin at virtual
BYTE address A. The normal T32 PC-operand
value for the instruction is A+4.

Take that value and clear its two lowest bits,
rounding DOWN to the nearest multiple of four:
    BASE = Align(A+4, 4).

Take the eight-bit literal imm8:
    BYTE_OFFSET = imm8*4.

Then the effective DATA MEMORY address is:
    WORD_ADDRESS = BASE + BYTE_OFFSET,
subject to the 32-bit address representation and
architecture/environment memory-access rules.

The word address is aligned by construction
under the ordinary ARMv7-A memory model.
This does NOT imply that the page is mapped,
readable, non-device memory, or even present.
The exact target's translation and access
permissions still apply.

The instruction requests one FOUR-BYTE
DATA MEMORY READ at WORD_ADDRESS.
If the memory access succeeds, the four
bytes are combined into one 32-bit
architectural register value according
to the target's applicable data-memory
endianness, and that value overwrites
the selected low register R0–R7.

The address computed in the arithmetic
is not automatically the value written
to the destination. The destination is
the value READ FROM MEMORY at the address,
not the address itself.

N/Z/C/V are not changed by the load.
SP and LR are unchanged. No store occurs.
The instruction does not itself branch,
and ordinary execution advances the
execution index from A to A+2.

When a legal IT block governs this
instruction, the data-load operation
is conditional upon its ITSTATE predicate;
a failed condition does not perform
the ordinary data-memory read or
destination register write.
The width still remains TWO bytes
for instruction sequencing, and
ITSTATE advances as architecturally
required.

An exception from data access is distinct
from ordinary successful completion.
A missing mapping or forbidden read
must not be converted into a zero-valued
load or into an instruction-execution event.

PART THREE. NUMERIC ALIGNMENT EXAMPLES.

Example starting instruction address A=0x1000:
   PC operand = 0x1004;
   Align(PC,4) = 0x1004.
   0x4800 selects R0, imm8=0;
   data word address = 0x1004.

Example starting instruction address A=0x1002:
   PC operand = 0x1006;
   Align(PC,4) = 0x1004.
   0x4800 again selects R0, imm8=0;
   data word address = 0x1004.
The two instructions have different
starting addresses and sequential successors,
but calculate the SAME memory word address.

At A=0x1002, 0x4901 means:
   destination R1;
   imm8=1;
   byte offset=4;
   data word address = 0x1008.

If mapped readable little-endian memory
contains bytes 78,56,34,12 at addresses
0x1008,0x1009,0x100A,0x100B, the load
produces R1=0x12345678.
It does NOT produce R1=0x1008.
The instruction still does not transfer
control to 0x1008.

The last encoding 0x4FFF selects R7,
imm8=255 and offset=1020 bytes.
At A=0x1000 the data word address is:
   0x1004+0x3FC = 0x1400.
Nothing in this encoding asserts that the
four bytes at 0x1400 are accessible.

PART FOUR. THIS IS NOT ADR.

Take an ADR T1 encoding A001,
executing at A=0x1002. It calculates:
   Align(0x1006,4) + 4 = 0x1008,
and WRITES the numerical address 0x1008
into R0. It does not access memory there.

Take an LDR literal T1 encoding 4801
executing at A=0x1002. It calculates
that SAME data address 0x1008 but
READS a 32-bit word from that address
and writes the word's DATA VALUE to R0.

For the same encoded immediate and
instruction location, the arithmetic
producing the address may agree.
The architectural operation does not.
One can fault on a data read;
the other can compute the numerical
address without dereferencing it.

Nor does either operation replace
the execution index. That is the
separate role of a branch B, BX,
BL, or other permitted control transfer.

PART FIVE. WORDS LOCATED IN AN EXECUTABLE SECTION MAY STILL BE DATA.

It is possible for a machine-code section
to include a mixture of native instructions
and literal words. An assembler's .word
directive may place an ordinary four-byte
data value among executable bytes.

An LDR literal may read that value as
DATA if the applicable memory mapping
allows a data read. A branch may skip
over it to a later instruction boundary.

A simple linear disassembler may still
decode those four bytes as two plausible
two-byte Thumb instructions if told
to start decoding at the literal's
address. That disassembler display
does NOT prove the processor ever
selected those bytes through its
architectural execution index.

One illustrative diagnostic ELF .text
section contains the exact 16 bytes:
    00 A0 01 49 02 E0 00 BE
    78 56 34 12 00 BF 01 DF

The data bytes 78 56 34 12 are an
explicit .word 0x12345678 at relative
offset eight. A linear LLVM decoder
instead prints their first halfword
as LDRSB and their second as ASRS.
The source assembler directives and
actual architectural control-flow
trace identify them as stored DATA,
not executed instructions on the
chosen path.

The checked English trace for that
exact specimen is in:
examples/An address a load and a branch disagree about the same bytes.english

This is a concrete reason that English
Major C's meaning cannot be obtained
from a mnemonic list alone. It must
account for current execution index,
memory effects, actual encoded length,
branches, and source-machine provenance.

PART SIX. ALTERNATIVES RETAINED IN THE DECISION HISTORY.

Alternative A: use T1 LDR literal to load
a data word from a nearby aligned address.
Benefit: short instruction, literal pool.
Cost: data access and its permission/fault
behavior, literal placement and execution
layout. This is the admitted T1 form.

Alternative B: use T1 ADR to produce the
literal word's ADDRESS, then use a separate
LDR register-offset or immediate form.
Benefit: address can be reused or
manipulated. Cost: extra instructions,
and the additional load remains
a distinct observed machine effect.

Alternative C: use MOVW and MOVT to build
the target value directly in a register.
Benefit: no literal data read.
Cost: different native instructions,
code size and relocation semantics.
A numerical equality for some input
does not make it the same disassembly.

Alternative D: use a wider LDR literal T32
form with longer or negative offsets.
Benefit: more available literal positions.
Cost: different encoding and operand rules;
not automatically granted by the narrow
LDR literal instruction name.

Alternative E: put literal data in a separate
read-only data region, rather than interleaved
with instructions. Benefit: clearer machine
provenance and possibly memory protection
requirements. Cost: address reach and
linkage layout may differ. The architecture
still uses the same data-read semantics.

Alternative F: use an executable section
that includes literal data and branch over it.
Benefit: compact PC-relative access on
some code layouts. Risk: a linear decoder
may misleadingly treat the literal bytes
as instructions. Correct inspection
requires actual code/data reachability.

Alternative G: treat the word at the literal
address as a machine instruction and begin
native execution there. This is a DIFFERENT
operation. It requires a legal control
transfer, a permitted instruction boundary
and executable memory. It is rejected
as a translation of the observed LDR.

Alternative H: define a typed source-level
pointer or capability associated with the
literal's address. This may enable a
future checker to enforce bounds and
permissions; it cannot retroactively
change the meaning of a raw T1 LDR
into a capability check performed
by the physical ARM instruction.

No alternative is said to be universally
optimal without actual target-specific
measurements or semantic evidence.

PART SEVEN. FINITE DIAGNOSTIC COVERAGE.

A temporary x86-64 diagnostic host used
clang 17.0.0 with target armv7a-none-eabi
in Thumb state to assemble every
halfword 4800..4FFF. LLVM objdump
17.0.0 decoded all 2048 into T1 LDR
literal operations. The independent
bitfield audit checked each raw halfword,
destination register, unsigned immediate,
scaled offset and instruction byte position.

An initial DIAGNOSTIC COMPARISON failed
for every row because the program
assumed the disassembler would print
decimal immediate values; LLVM instead
printed hexadecimal values with 0x prefix.
The diagnostic was repaired to parse
both representations as NUMBERS rather
than comparing incompatible text strings.

After that correction, all 2048
rows matched and there were ZERO
remaining differences. The original
failure is retained here rather than
being erased or called a semantic
machine-architecture error.

The assembler/disassembler did not
execute a memory read. Its output is
instruction-decoding evidence only.
The initial English Major C language
does not have an accepted compiler,
instruction interpreter or device run
from this diagnostic.

PART EIGHT. REQUIRED INDEPENDENT REJECTION TESTS.

Reject an LDR translation that:
  1. writes the ADDRESS rather than
     the fetched 32-bit DATA value;
  2. uses unaligned A+4 in place
     of Align(A+4,4) at A=0x1002;
  3. omits four-byte scaling of imm8;
  4. treats the eight-bit immediate
     as a signed branch displacement;
  5. permits R8, SP, LR or PC as T1
     destination when its three bits
     select only R0..R7;
  6. changes N/Z/C/V as though
     performing a comparison;
  7. treats the destination word
     as an executed native instruction;
  8. assumes a successful data load
     from an address that is not
     established readable/mapped;
  9. performs a data load even though
     a legal enclosing IT predicate
     is false;
 10. advances the execution index
     four bytes after a two-byte T1;
 11. treats the literal's memory bytes
     as proof that a linear disassembler's
     displayed instructions were executed;
 12. substitutes ADR, a branch, or
     immediate construction as the
     original observed native operation;
 13. claims 2048 decode rows establish
     successful data-memory executions;
 14. claims all Thumb/ARM instructions
     have been enumerated.

Source decoding and the transient
diagnostic comparison are complete
for this finite region. Full accepted
runtime meaning and a target OS
memory map are not complete.

END OF 4800..4FFF LDR LITERAL CONTRACT.
